rcles and flux densities from the literature are shown as open circles. Posterior realisations are shown as the grey shaded region with the posterior median indicated as the black dashed line. For GSS30-IRS1 and DoAr24Eb, we use a single power law fit to the radio SED and so only present α \alpha . Spectral indices for α dust \alpha_{	ext{dust}} are shown in the top left and α ionised \alpha_{	ext{ionised}} is shown in the bottom right. To decide which model most accurately represents the data whilst avoiding overfitting we employ the Akaike information criterion (AIC, Akaike 1 ). The AIC balances a model’s goodness of fit to its complexity, implementing a penalty for more complex models. We calculate the pairwise differences in AIC, defined as Δ ​ AIC = AIC complex − AIC simple \Delta	ext{AIC}=	ext{AIC}_{	ext{complex}}-	ext{AIC}_{	ext{simple}} , such that negative values indicate support for the more complex model. Following the criteria described in Burnham and Anderson [25] , we choose the simplest, single power law model unless substantial support ( Δ \Delta AIC < − 4 <-4 ) was found for the double power law model. As the broken power law model does not account for the full data range, it was only selected when there was strong support ( Δ \Delta AIC < − 10 <-10 ) relative to both single and double power law models. We find only two objects, GSS30-IRS1 and DoAr24Eb, to be consistent with the single power law model, each with a spectral index less than 2. We present the results of our radio SED fitting in Figure 5 . Considering both the double and broken power law models we find that all objects except GSS30-IRS2 have α dust ≤ 2.3 \alpha_{	ext{dust}}\leq 2.3 indicating either dust growth or potentially optically thick emission at (sub-)mm wavelengths. GSS30-IRS2 has α dust = 3.1 \alpha_{	ext{dust}}=3.1 , potentially indicating a comparatively lower level of dust growth and lower optical depth. However, it is important to note the high level of uncertainty associated with GSS30-IRS2 ( ∼ ± 0.5 \sim\pm 0.5 ). The second highest α dust \alpha_{	ext{dust}} comes from GSS26 which has been found to have ring and gap features [ 31 ] . Excluding GSS30-IRS2, we find that α dust \alpha_{	ext{dust}} is on average consistent across evolutionary stages going from 2.2 ± \pm 0.1 for Class 0 to 2.1 ± \pm 0.1 and 2.1 ± \pm 0.2 for Class I and II respectively. Spectral indices approaching 2 indicate that in general, our sample contains optically thick emission, primarily in the (sub-)mm. For the ionised gas component we find that, on average, Class I objects have the highest α ionised ∼ 0.4 ± 0.5 \alpha_{	ext{ionised}}\sim 0.4\,\pm\,0.5 . Whereas, Class 0 and II objects have typically more negative spectral indices with α ionised = \alpha_{	ext{ionised}}= − - 0.2 ± \pm 0.4 and − - 0.2 ± \pm 0.2, respectively. The difference in α ionised \alpha_{	ext{ionised}} may show evidence of varying dominant emission mechanisms with evolutionary stage, with Class II objects being more dominated by non-thermal gyro-emission and Class I objects being more consistent with thermal free-free emission from jets or winds [ 42 , 9 ] . However, given such high uncertainties this trend is purely speculative and cannot be confidently confirmed. Using the spectral index we can estimate the contribution of each emission component to the total flux density in order to understand which frequencies are most dominated by dust emission, and which are dominated by ionised gas emission. In Table 2 we present the fraction of emission contributed by ionised gas, f ion {}_{	ext{ion}} , at a range of commonly observed frequencies from ALMA (218 GHz) to VLA (10 GHz). We derive f ion {}_{	ext{ion}} by comparing the total flux density predicted by the model to the flux density contributed solely from the ionised gas component. We note that values derived from the broken power law model will underestimate the true fraction of contaminating emission at high and low frequencies, as we estimate α ionised \alpha_{	ext{ionised}} using all VLA flux densities. As can be seen from the double power law sample, there is a large range of contamination values at 44 GHz ranging from ∼ 1 % \sim 1\% up to 68 % 68\% in the most extreme case. If we consider the fraction of ionised emission at 10 GHz, we find that all but one object (VLA 1623 Ab) have f ion > 70 % {}_{	ext{ion}}>70\% . As expected, emission at ALMA frequencies (218 and 100 GHz) is typically dominated by emission from dust over ionised gas processes. Conversely, emission at 10 GHz primarily originates from ionised gas mechanisms as found previously [ 35 , 61 , 128 , e.g.] . Interestingly, if we consider the average properties of the double power law and broken power law sample, we find that more evolved objects tend to have higher fractions of non-dust emission at low frequencies. Class 0 objects have f ion = 11 ± 6 % {}_{	ext{ion}}=11\pm 6\% and 55 ± 8 % 55\pm 8\% at 44 GHz and 10 GHz, respectively, whereas Class II objects have f ion = 23 ± 8 {}_{	ext{ion}}=23\pm 8 % and 74 ± 8 74\pm 8 % for the same frequencies. Finally, while our dust spectral indices are relatively well constrained, the ionised gas spectral index and, therefore f ion {}_{	ext{ion}} , remains largely uncertain across the majority of our sample. This uncertainty arises primarily from increased variability and lower angular resolutions at frequencies < 10 <10 GHz, resulting in potentially unreliable flux density measurements. To overcome this, future surveys should incorporate contemporaneous observations spanning the 1-40 GHz range, enabling an instantaneous characterisation of the low frequency spectral index. This could be achieved through a coordinated effort combining A-configuration VLA observations, as presented here, with SKA-MID for frequencies < 10 <10 GHz, or alternatively through the ngVLA alone, which would provide comparable spectral coverage and increased resolution across its full frequency range [ 24 , 147 ] .

While planet formation in protoplanetary disks is now well established [ 77 , 161 , e.g.] , there appears to be an observational disconnect between inferred dust masses and the masses of exoplanets [ 110 , e.g.] . Comparisons of the available dust mass in Class II disks and solid content of exoplanetary systems reveal a significant deficit, necessitating an almost 100% planet formation efficiency [ 107 , 98 , e.g.] . This discrepancy is often referred to as the ‘missing mass’ or ‘mass budget’ problem. However, these dust masses were derived using ALMA observations which in some cases have been found to be optically thick [ 16 , 133 , 137 , 171 , 29 , 128 , e.g.] , therefore leading to reduced disk masses. Additionally, neglecting dust scattering can lead to a reduction in mass by a factor of 10 for compact ( < 30 <30 au) disks [ 177 , e.g.] . Given that our radio SED analysis indicates that a portion of the millimetre emission may be optically thick (e.g. α dust ∼ 2 \alpha_{	ext{dust}}\sim 2 ) or potentially impacted by dust self-scattering [ 149 , e.g. α dust ∼ 1.5 \alpha_{	ext{dust}}\sim 1.5 ,] in half of our sample, our multiwavelength analysis will be able to investigate the impact of such effects. Our SED-derived dust masses enable us to directly quantify the bias introduced from single wavelength mass estimates, which may be significantly impacted by optical depth effects. In order to compare our results to previous approaches we use the standard dust mass equation from Hildebrand [66] : M dust = F ν ​ D 2 κ ν ​ B ν ​ ( T dust ) M_{m dust}= rac{F_{
