u, eta} of ∼ 1.8 \sim 1.8 cm 2 g -1 at 1.3 mm whereas κ ν , DSHARP \kappa_{
u,m DSHARP} has an average of ∼ 11 \sim 11 cm 2 g -1 . As M dust ∝ κ ν − 1 M_{m dust}\propto\kappa_{
u}^{-1} this order of magnitude difference results in much closer agreement between the masses inferred from the radio SED and those inferred from 1.3 mm flux densities when using the Beckwith et al. [18] opacity law. Nevertheless, both opacity laws produce reduced dust masses compared to our multiwavelength approach. The significant increase in SED-derived dust masses relative to those derived from flux densities at 1.3 mm may have several explanations. The most significant of which is high optical depths at millimetre wavelengths, as reported across a number of Class 0–II disks [ 177 , 29 ] . For the majority of our sample, we find dust spectral indices ≤ 2.3 \leq 2.3 , consistent with the presence of optically thick millimetre emission, supporting high optical depths as a contributing factor [ 55 ] . Additionally, since the emissivity of dust grains peak at λ ∼ 2 ​ π ​ a max \lambda\sim 2\pi a_{m max} , millimetre observations are less sensitive to cm-sized grains, potentially excluding a portion of the mass reservoir [ 38 , 166 ] . Observations with ALMA Band 6 are therefore most sensitive to the emission of dust grains of the order 0.2 mm, yet we infer grain growth beyond this for all objects in our sample. This suggests that a fraction of the dust mass may be unaccounted for in the mass derived from the 1.3 mm flux density. Previous comparisons of dust masses derived from SEDs and masses derived from millimetre flux densities have reported underestimations up to a factor of ∼ \sim 6, with the discrepancy increasing for more massive disks [ 95 , 171 , 137 , e.g.] . However, these studies did not include cm-wavelength observations and thus, may be insensitive to emission from larger grains representing a lower limit to the discrepancy. Consistent with this, Painter et al. [118] found an order of magnitude increase in the available dust mass when comparing optically thin 40 40 GHz emission to optically thick emission at 340 GHz. Our inclusion of cm-wave observations, therefore, allows us to utilise optically thin tracers and, increase our sensitivity to larger dust grains, providing a more complete picture of the available dust mass. Despite the large discrepancies of inferred masses in each method, our radio SED-derived dust mass estimates remain physically plausible. Previous multiwavelength approaches have inferred dust disk masses between 250–2000 M ⊕ M_{\oplus} for Class II disks [ 95 , 61 , 60 , e.g.] . In our sample, the derived masses for Class II disks are typically below 200 M ⊕ M_{\oplus} with DoAr24Ea having the highest inferred dust mass of 390 M ⊕ M_{\oplus} , occupying the lower end of the currently observed range. In addition, several of our Class 0 and I objects (VLA 1623AaAb,B,W, and SM1) have masses comparable to those found by Maureira et al. [100] . Class 0 and I objects are generally expected to have higher disk masses than Class II objects as they have had less time to deplete their circumstellar disk through accretion [ 172 ] . However, with the exception of GSS30-IRS3, the dust masses we infer for Class 0-I objects are typically ≲ 500 \lesssim 500 M ⊕ M_{\oplus} , once again in agreement with previous multiwavelength dust mass estimates. In summary, we find that our derived disk parameters and masses represent a physically plausible model of the observed radio SED emission. The substantially increased dust masses relative to those derived from millimetre flux densities alone can be explained by the combined effects of high optical depths, dust growth and the inclusion of cm-wave observations in our analysis. While these masses are indeed larger than estimates from millimetre flux densities, they primarily occupy the lower range of previous SED-derived dust masses, providing confidence in their reliability [ 95 , 61 , 118 , e.g.] . Consequently, these results have significant implications for planet formation and the missing mass problem. Masses inferred from millimetre flux densities using DSHARP opacities typically lack the sufficient solid material to build Earth-like cores, with the majority unable to achieve the 10 M ⊕ M_{\oplus} threshold required for runaway gas accretion to form Jupiter-like planets [ 105 , 124 ] . This is consistent with the previously reported ‘missing mass’ problem [ 107 , 98 , e.g.] . Alternatively, the Beckwith et al. [18] opacity prescription yields a higher average dust mass of ∼ \sim 40 M ⊕ M_{\oplus} , which while beneficial for planet formation, would require very high planet formation efficiencies to create multi-planet systems [ 108 , e.g. 100%] . Therefore, our radio SED-derived dust masses present a partial alleviation to this tension, with all objects in our sample containing sufficient solid material to form multiple gas giant cores, with residual mass available to form smaller rocky bodies even through inefficient processes.

